Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[" Exercises  6.5", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.3986012958653307`*^9, 
  3.3986013045528307`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"51fc5870-2a94-4258-b596-\
ea43290cf0a0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
The struggle for life, part II
Section 6.3\
\>", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a5f2dbb6-e404-42d2-88b6-68df40065e66"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Part 1: Perform a linear stability analysis on the example illustrated in \
the Mathematica notebook for Section 6.3 (use the values given in the \
Mathematica notebook for Section 6.3 for all other model parameters and do \
this ",
 StyleBox["only",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " for the case \[Alpha] = 100 and for the equilibrium point \
{x2\[Rule]29.4202,  x1\[Rule]226.309}).  The analysis should follow the \
procedure developed in previous Mathematica notebooks and not just use the \
formula developed in the book.  \n\nPart 2:  The objective of this exercise \
is to determine the value of alpha which divides the behavior of the \
predator-prey model into:\n\n\t an unstable equilibrium with a stable limit \
cycle, and\n\n\t a stable equilibrium without a limit cycle.\n\nExcept for \
the value of alpha, use the values given in the Mathematica notebook for \
Section 6.3 for all other model parameters.\nNote! The location of the \
equilibrium point changes location as a function of \[Alpha]. Therefore, you \
must first determine the new location of the equilibrium point corresponding \
to the one which is  {x2\[Rule]29.4202,  x1\[Rule]226.309} when \[Alpha] = \
100. Then your linear stability analysis will be performed using this \
equilibrium point location."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2d15e66e-4a3e-4bc4-ab1e-cb8cbf7fc516"]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]]
